torname{sin}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ &=2 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ &=\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244088 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "419fbda5-ea39-5bfc-8620-cc182f000989" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+{30}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241769} {:answer/id #uuid "8673b6f9-f305-57aa-a3da-3798105dccd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+{60}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242359} {:answer/id #uuid "b142571f-e6f5-57ea-bc1e-c3ec1c8dd0c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+{60}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242723} {:answer/id #uuid "cb449938-9332-56b0-9eff-2e784a030f94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+{60}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242970} {:answer/id #uuid "fc60dcd0-0116-5c41-a6dc-f553b90f74ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+{30}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "419fbda5-ea39-5bfc-8620-cc182f000989" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+{30}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241769} :answer-field/id #uuid "5af43c0e-a61c-50ae-bb48-b0ac6b82527e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243620}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4f29e134-3f34-58a8-8500-c5b6c8981089" :question/math-academy-id "q-107571" :question/problem "$\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=$" :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ &=\\sqrt{6} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ &=\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\ ϕ & ={30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & =(\\sqrt{6})^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =6+2 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =8 \\\\ R & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+{30}^{∘})\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244089 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cdc0764-c704-5f4c-8900-21357c4cc0a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+0.730)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241721} {:answer/id #uuid "4b4c9039-c2fb-5ec2-94bc-d45cd5379b7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+0.841)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241858} {:answer/id #uuid "786d7e6b-a9ac-5d0e-ae28-08ad63146c2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+0.741)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242240} {:answer/id #uuid "870281f1-0101-507d-854d-e412a542cbbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+0.841)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242361} {:answer/id #uuid "bbc5f724-7da5-5411-9f8c-9ba0486c21de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+0.730)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242824}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cdc0764-c704-5f4c-8900-21357c4cc0a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+0.730)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241721} :answer-field/id #uuid "9923f324-3fc1-5d64-bb7b-b26016552565" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243724}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e19c29ed-ca08-5b08-881a-c2cea1937130" :question/math-academy-id "q-107574" :question/problem "$\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x≈$" :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ &=\\sqrt{5} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}) \\\\ ϕ & ≈0.730\\,\\text{rad}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{R}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ & =(\\sqrt{5})^{2}+{2}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =5+4 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =9 \\\\ R & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x≈3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+0.730)\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244091 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "065cf14a-cb11-5884-91db-27d556be5c48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241238} {:answer/id #uuid "3ab1e495-9abf-5dcc-9ba0-2c366b94fe5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3+\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241703} {:answer/id #uuid "6f64f3cb-15a5-5bbf-9555-bf0c46bd6405" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242172} {:answer/id #uuid "ba9c81d3-0863-56e5-b925-874b7bcdd68a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242816} {:answer/id #uuid "d71eed36-af61-5de5-a12a-8895cf85a29a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243089}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba9c81d3-0863-56e5-b925-874b7bcdd68a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242816} :answer-field/id #uuid "02a0c3bc-b0d0-57d7-b88b-664cefeda39b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243441}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "771ab1fb-0873-549b-b5d0-5795adc724e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-107578" :question/problem "Express $z=3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}))$ in the form $z=x+\\text{i}y.$" :question/worked-solution "To express the number in Cartesian form, we expand the parentheses and substitute the values of the cosine and sine.\n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=0$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=-1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =3(0-\\text{i}) \\\\  & =-3\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244092 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13ef2485-088a-59e5-a9d3-d6d81ac05af0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241351} {:answer/id #uuid "4579478f-975c-5a82-9cbc-e216916864ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241804} {:answer/id #uuid "a47542a8-9b3c-5c1f-a990-6c080eb2c3cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242627} {:answer/id #uuid "b9c37fb5-15bc-5c9c-ac02-81147abd5673" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242810} {:answer/id #uuid "ee8cf53f-c6cd-5298-a246-3f874d8397d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243295}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13ef2485-088a-59e5-a9d3-d6d81ac05af0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241351} :answer-field/id #uuid "76907d51-a76b-59d2-814f-949dc43b9da2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b5b0b758-0715-5d36-8c00-3c3db2730695" :question/math-academy-id "q-107579" :question/problem "Given that the expression $2\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ)$ with $0<ϕ<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $R>0$, what is the value of $ϕ?$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for cosine gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ &=2\\sqrt{6} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ &=2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{2\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\ ϕ & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244100 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15ffbc91-6330-5e11-a7e0-8ad693861a08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241369} {:answer/id #uuid "7471c1f3-71ef-593d-bfe4-4c431ae08341" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242213} {:answer/id #uuid "8ff24a3f-b973-55aa-be2f-c74c1c177474" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242443} {:answer/id #uuid "c9eda060-255c-5b3e-987a-ac187ca80bfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242962} {:answer/id #uuid "ff97e295-b477-589f-a962-c7c41d1e14ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243430}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9eda060-255c-5b3e-987a-ac187ca80bfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242962} :answer-field/id #uuid "81abe7a0-2e0a-5385-a458-f0a35f5883a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243687}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ad3752e9-ac56-5e6a-a8ab-a41a282342a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-10769" :question/problem "Find the radius of convergence $R$ for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{n}^{2}(x+1)^{n}=(x+1)+{2}^{2}(x+1)^{2}+{3}^{2}(x+1)^{3}+⋯\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test. we define ${a}_{n}={n}^{2}(x+1)^{n}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(n+1)^{2}(x+1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}(x+1)^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(n+1)^{2}(x+1)}{{n}^{2}}| \\\\  & =|x+1|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{n+1}{n})}^{2} \\\\  & =|x+1|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =|x+1|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied if $|x+1|<1.$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244105 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ce3a1b4-a3cb-50bf-b683-6273efe59b61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x-y+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241424} {:answer/id #uuid "550dce1b-1d12-5c00-bf63-e187f3cc82c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241946} {:answer/id #uuid "64af1c39-4794-55cb-9588-01a284e589ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+y+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242084} {:answer/id #uuid "78a345f7-f9a1-528b-92c0-afaefd79fa28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+y-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242242} {:answer/id #uuid "a3f387dc-ce71-5141-93b8-e3126bdabe76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+y-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242618}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78a345f7-f9a1-528b-92c0-afaefd79fa28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+y-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242242} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6fa166b-2a28-5db4-b15a-93030703eeb9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243813}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d0db4ae1-7ca8-5d44-90b5-79a2a42ad1ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-107815" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2x+y}{3-2x-y}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡|g(x,y)|=-\\frac{x+y}{3}+C\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x+y}{3-(2x+y)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=2x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2+\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2 & =\\frac{z}{3-z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{6-z}{3-z}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{3-z}{6-z})\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int (\\frac{z-3}{z-6})\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (\\frac{3}{z-6}+1)\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|z-6|+z & =x+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+y-6|+2x+y & =x+K \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+y-6| & =-\\frac{x+y}{3}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=\\frac{K}{3}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=2x+y-6.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244113 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2985b397-f2ad-57bc-baa0-56103fcbe28d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241545} {:answer/id #uuid "3c885850-b720-5910-8481-3b8fc8554e4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241719} {:answer/id #uuid "4f1cd1b1-11b8-5693-9e6a-6325ded9f659" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241886} {:answer/id #uuid "d396b976-5f63-57a4-868f-2a52795a54ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243049} {:answer/id #uuid "f60345e9-7d23-527d-97be-25d556e71c3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243357}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f1cd1b1-11b8-5693-9e6a-6325ded9f659" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241886} :answer-field/id #uuid "c49997ef-2ad8-5ac6-8a41-2278890403d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243786}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d85fab59-8e48-543a-b7a0-bd15b756ce1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-108008" :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda =-5$ and $\\lambda =6$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 9 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =-5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 9 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}-(-5)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}9{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{2}{9}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For $\\lambda =6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 9 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}-6\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 9 \\\\ 2 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-9{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -9 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{9}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{9}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244114 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07e2e056-bdc3-5fa4-a393-7c34a4777ea0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241244} {:answer/id #uuid "0f8eaad2-644e-5de1-8b21-7effd3c6925d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241313} {:answer/id #uuid "481fcfe4-3c47-53dc-8c1f-806723491f6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241832} {:answer/id #uuid "7b90051e-5f6c-509e-a985-fc6754f1457f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242265} {:answer/id #uuid "e2fa7992-7dde-5610-b276-4f858472ac17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f8eaad2-644e-5de1-8b21-7effd3c6925d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241313} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc56ee31-2bc8-5029-af18-96fe3c9c2a56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243796}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e9180a9d-8bb8-5d9a-bdf7-f88ca64062d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-108039" :question/problem "If $v=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =7,$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =7.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-7\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 9 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(\\frac{2}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-3{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=3{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{1}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244127 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0abcd17f-952e-5ec6-8fc1-e15b19233f81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)+3{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241277} {:answer/id #uuid "7f524026-bb7b-5692-aa92-5df2f0fa376e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4x}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)-3y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242299} {:answer/id #uuid "c0fb17d5-81be-5f73-8b4b-2fbb56c7c484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)-{y}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242868} {:answer/id #uuid "d7e220dc-2da0-5e71-8e92-14bd79bb0955" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4x}{3{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243098} {:answer/id #uuid "e7ff3c6a-3127-59cf-8abb-ff7552021965" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4x}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243235}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7e220dc-2da0-5e71-8e92-14bd79bb0955" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4x}{3{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243098} :answer-field/id #uuid "5685c02e-3a96-5d71-9a03-4ca4b0ff6731" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243612}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "673feb27-4ae3-559c-903e-378e7e84b56d" :question/math-academy-id "q-108367" :question/problem "Given that ${y}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)-2{x}^{2}=5,$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{y}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)-2{x}^{2} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)-2{x}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y))-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})-4x & =0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}(3{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)) & =4x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{4x}{3{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244140 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0384f4a9-12fe-5387-a1be-f88c7e01239b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241211} {:answer/id #uuid "129626b4-e403-5da1-8705-0a1f6a0c1510" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6x}{y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241340} {:answer/id #uuid "81f2495e-8df6-56e4-90ed-bfeeda18086e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)\\,xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242320} {:answer/id #uuid "c2bf0798-5176-5064-907f-27ffcf873486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242890} {:answer/id #uuid "cf706940-1972-5b4d-8306-565d30014034" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)\\,xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243011}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81f2495e-8df6-56e4-90ed-bfeeda18086e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)\\,xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242320} :answer-field/id #uuid "40437dbb-8029-5af3-8c3b-5299eddeaf71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243564}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ee119daa-efcf-505f-b183-08b031d8df4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-108475" :question/problem "Given that $\\operatorname{log}⁡(y)+3{x}^{2}=11,$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(y)+3{x}^{2} & =11 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{log}⁡(y)+3{x}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(11) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{log}⁡(y))+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})+6x & =0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-6x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(-6x)(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)\\,xy\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244147 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05a2311e-18e7-5113-971f-2b522819e922" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241226} {:answer/id #uuid "377165d9-847c-5e0b-8161-73ba159b514a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241668} {:answer/id #uuid "5c6fc430-b7f6-5860-9bc6-f537f9f529e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242013} {:answer/id #uuid "9ec3f5d6-c0ee-50ec-9327-3fc7782b7910" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242571} {:answer/id #uuid "acd8443d-84d8-5126-9fa2-4a78e29505a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242689}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c6fc430-b7f6-5860-9bc6-f537f9f529e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242013} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1f21cfc-8a2d-58bc-85dc-1cde3c3f79ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "efe44e24-3349-54e3-ae25-0cd4ad9b52a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-108637" :question/problem "What is the first term of an arithmetic series if the common difference is $d=\\frac{5}{7},$ and the sum of the first $50$ terms is ${S}_{50}=1\\,125?$" :question/worked-solution "We are given the common difference of the series. So, to find the first term, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series in the form\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, $d$ is the common difference, and $N$ is the number of terms.\n\nSubstituting $N=50$, $d=\\frac{5}{7}$, and ${S}_{N}=1\\,125$ into the formula above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1\\,125 & =\\frac{50}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(50-1)(\\frac{5}{7})) \\\\ 1\\,125 & =25(2{a}_{1}+35) \\\\ 45 & =2{a}_{1}+35 \\\\ 10 & =2{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244152 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45ce074c-f5ee-5790-955f-744688452a51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241807} {:answer/id #uuid "7e670689-9386-512c-80a1-9ffb0405857d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242290} {:answer/id #uuid "a4fe8801-fc93-5cb1-b234-3374bb79395a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242633} {:answer/id #uuid "d4b24f33-d38a-5c21-a5c3-2817021cafc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243063} {:answer/id #uuid "de835654-3bb0-5b6e-95d2-3def6a996d9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243159}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45ce074c-f5ee-5790-955f-744688452a51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241807} :answer-field/id #uuid "504aa2d6-12d2-5a6b-8911-82cd37da7948" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243594}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ffb47def-243e-5613-87da-6b6ae8fdf163" :question/math-academy-id "q-108674" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,2)$ for the curve $y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\ y\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) & =0 \\\\ y(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,2)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}_{(\\pi /3,\\,2)} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)})}_{(\\pi /3,\\,2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244153 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e607f78-385d-53f6-ab41-74ac877a5a71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241591} {:answer/id #uuid "32897127-ad14-5eef-9a81-301deb3e2a1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241624} {:answer/id #uuid "d18a655d-56b1-5123-a490-d7bf85825ed6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243025} {:answer/id #uuid "dc7ba673-b558-5e38-9cf1-92dadf966d68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243142} {:answer/id #uuid "fd113b97-7abf-5d30-9cb0-076358176a03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32897127-ad14-5eef-9a81-301deb3e2a1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241624} :answer-field/id #uuid "52dfb695-349e-5936-9fe3-1ad1eeda4511" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243598}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "85cec444-2f0a-598c-bff4-89c6e3584a08" :question/math-academy-id "q-108679" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $(4,\\,4)$ for the curve $\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y}=4.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{1/2}+{y}^{1/2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{y}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(4,\\,4)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}_{(4,\\,4)} \\\\  & =-{\\frac{\\sqrt{y}}{\\sqrt{x}}}_{(4,\\,4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{4}}{\\sqrt{4}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{2} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244183 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a488a6a-c562-5116-a808-9606ad5fcd65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{2}^{2x+y}}{2x+y+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241996} {:answer/id #uuid "6f9114ac-eb6e-5022-a9dc-0dfb399af7d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{2}^{2x+y}}{2x+y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242175} {:answer/id #uuid "bfe35dec-6d42-59f5-8111-b8e850cce8b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{2}^{2x+y}(2x+y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242858} {:answer/id #uuid "cb622b0b-0e9a-5e4a-8f28-6fb1f1e8c0dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{2}^{2x+y}(2x+y-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242971} {:answer/id #uuid "e106123d-d9ac-599d-b55f-445e9e4d1b79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{2}^{2x+y}(2x+y-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243180}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e106123d-d9ac-599d-b55f-445e9e4d1b79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{2}^{2x+y}(2x+y-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243180} :answer-field/id #uuid "579ac401-6a63-5a61-82b5-87d55aa593a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243616}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fe2488e6-1056-5c66-a150-25aaef8df7c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-108858" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{(2x+y)\\,{2}^{2x+y}}-2.\n$$\n\n \nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=2x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2 & =\\frac{1}{z\\,{2}^{z}}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{z\\,{2}^{z}}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\,{2}^{z}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int z\\,{2}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing integration by parts on the left side,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=z & \\,⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={2}^{z} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\frac{{2}^{z}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int z\\,{2}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{z\\,{2}^{z}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\int {2}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{z\\,{2}^{z}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(\\frac{{2}^{z}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}) & =x+C \\\\ \\frac{{2}^{z}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(z-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}) & =x+K \\\\ {2}^{z}(z-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}) & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=K\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{2x+y}(2x+y-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}) & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x,y)={2}^{2x+y}(2x+y-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244195 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25a64257-013e-5f8d-b152-d9e7634b93e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461521} {:answer/id #uuid "44f88025-dd1b-5198-a95d-7ba9a5e96e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25a64257-013e-5f8d-b152-d9e7634b93e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461521} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4513080-f5de-5d9a-b8bd-7e3281ab4ff6" :answer-field/key "${y}^{′}-{e}^{x}{y}^{2}=\\sqrt{y}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462514} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "002f52a6-9b40-5817-806e-04c903f93628" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461523} {:answer/id #uuid "f65c2b84-64a9-52f6-9a3b-93f218800c5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461524}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f65c2b84-64a9-52f6-9a3b-93f218800c5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461524} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4d9626a-f524-53e7-923d-73b8234b228b" :answer-field/key "${y}^{3}{y}^{′}+{y}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=\\sqrt{y}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462515} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e652a4bd-a6e3-5008-b2e6-7bbfef7cb7ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461525} {:answer/id #uuid "eda70d64-65fd-54ee-82af-095577f34912" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461526}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e652a4bd-a6e3-5008-b2e6-7bbfef7cb7ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461525} :answer-field/id #uuid "83fc44fd-f73d-55e3-a891-ad000c8c31b2" :answer-field/key "$y\\,{y}^{′}+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y={cos}^{2}⁡y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462516}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d4b7e54f-2216-5cba-ba81-1d5ec0ebc609" :question/math-academy-id "q-108918" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a real number.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation ${y}^{′}-{e}^{x}{y}^{2}=\\sqrt{y}$ is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing the $p(x)y$ term.\n\n- Equation ${y}^{3}{y}^{′}+{y}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=\\sqrt{y}$ is a Bernoulli differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)y={y}^{-5/2}\n$$\n\nwhich is a Bernoulli differential equation with\n\n$$\np(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,\\,q(x)=1,\\,n=-\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\n- Equation $y\\,{y}^{′}+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y={cos}^{2}⁡y$ is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation.  If we attempt to write the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+x(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}{y})=\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡y}{y}.\n$$\n\nIt is missing both the $p(x)y$ and $q(x){y}^{n}$ terms.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{3}{y}^{′}+{y}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=\\sqrt{y}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244219 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2db67a4b-551b-50d3-aa9d-94998480bd57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{x}{C{e}^{x/2}-1})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241586} {:answer/id #uuid "46aaacb9-f86c-5ffc-ac24-3c6368262bf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(C{e}^{x/2}-1)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241818} {:answer/id #uuid "51b19b15-add7-5c7e-8009-0bf343fb02a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{C{e}^{2x}+x+1}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241910} {:answer/id #uuid "d004786f-80c4-57b9-b27c-963af74bb669" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{{e}^{2x}+C}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243012} {:answer/id #uuid "fb1e0800-7084-5f24-ae7c-61d79f458bb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={({e}^{x/2}-2x+C)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243393}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46aaacb9-f86c-5ffc-ac24-3c6368262bf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(C{e}^{x/2}-1)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241818} :answer-field/id #uuid "74c6f198-e482-574d-8ea3-6f80192a57d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243667}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f2cab3ea-07f6-5b6d-87cc-2af665f5707a" :question/math-academy-id "q-109029" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-y=\\sqrt{y}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-1,\\,q(x)=1,\\,n=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{1/2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-1/2}\\frac{dy}{dx}-{y}^{1/2}=1.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{1/2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-1/2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-z=1\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{2}z=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (-1/2)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={e}^{-x/2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x/2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{2}z) & ={e}^{-x/2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ {e}^{-x/2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{2}z{e}^{-x/2} & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x/2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{-x/2}) & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{-x/2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z{e}^{-x/2} & =-{e}^{-x/2}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =C{e}^{x/2}-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C={C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{1/2}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{1/2}=C{e}^{x/2}-1\\,⇒\\,y={(C{e}^{x/2}-1)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244234 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "295fa52a-dd9e-5690-a223-d5740988ec4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241543} {:answer/id #uuid "4e055f25-296b-5f8a-a9fe-58748fa4bdc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241880} {:answer/id #uuid "c26640c7-6309-583d-85c9-feb0f05808dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242884} {:answer/id #uuid "c3d3a980-c436-5cf1-a7be-8010557a2d98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242900} {:answer/id #uuid "d1610221-8616-54dc-924e-d4d56d02261a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243024}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c26640c7-6309-583d-85c9-feb0f05808dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242884} :answer-field/id #uuid "e276da50-ba57-5a33-82d4-3648f7a5c230" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243829}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dece3370-94a9-5933-91ea-e0822d5c845c" :question/math-academy-id "q-109124" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ that lies between the lines $x=1$ and $x=e.$ What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${x}_{1}=1$ to ${x}_{2}=e.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{1}{x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244235 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "085cbd66-a8a1-5980-be31-57eed7767d00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-{x}^{2})\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241251} {:answer/id #uuid "2f3fdd79-1ab3-5d0a-b7ab-a5b2e8c412a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241598} {:answer/id #uuid "6b4d1b73-4c0e-5624-bc18-d2e1c35263f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242145} {:answer/id #uuid "74c28729-d2fa-57e5-b80e-b90730cc0451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242220} {:answer/id #uuid "ad3fb0b2-6ed8-5f12-8325-ba7b3c04569a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242695}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f3fdd79-1ab3-5d0a-b7ab-a5b2e8c412a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241598} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd3abb33-a3b3-5c9b-a1d6-ca3895f498d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243799}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "815b3056-c4c0-522e-992e-21d3b5a5f29d" :question/math-academy-id "q-109126" :question/problem "![](images/0e/0edb5218c2d1527957053a35e992c7538a8857132a6d48114e16e5f71d3848d6.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $y=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}$ that lies in the first quadrant between the line $x=0$ and its $x$-intercept. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 4-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}})\\sqrt{1+{(-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244236 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23d8c066-7867-5eac-9b9c-1696e4efd675" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241492} {:answer/id #uuid "36db06cb-9213-5e4b-8216-0dea5618ec38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241663} {:answer/id #uuid "53428bc9-6dd9-5fe3-9c0f-9fdc6c63f8f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241925} {:answer/id #uuid "c127b9bd-45f4-56f2-b3b5-d08e61034189" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242873} {:answer/id #uuid "f50b50e7-49d5-52e4-8ac0-dfb07852b27a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f50b50e7-49d5-52e4-8ac0-dfb07852b27a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243345} :answer-field/id #uuid "c72e9f53-402d-51aa-8ec3-8bb61e794fdf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "791b6409-d8b7-533d-ab9f-9fd6eeb81e0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-109130" :question/problem "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,2),(1,0)$ and $(2,0),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})\\,dx+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nCalculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/8e/8e3efc3f9c58fb0f9a84144e97a300b6ab2a4011d47ee0d2bae1edc8a3f721cd.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})\\,dx+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244237 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f66b5c3-572f-56d8-b763-9956cb256230" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241312} {:answer/id #uuid "13c6828e-9aa5-57a5-898e-af3e5b35e609" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}(2-\\sqrt{x})\\sqrt{\\frac{4x+1}{x}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241347} {:answer/id #uuid "1c1ede2e-8b96-53f6-b2e6-622c87007c08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}(2-\\sqrt{x})\\sqrt{\\frac{4x+1}{x}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241418} {:answer/id #uuid "5109198d-edfe-5357-a55a-643391f8be90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{(2-x)\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+1}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241904} {:answer/id #uuid "66e36fa5-59aa-5c9d-835e-06208c5f6974" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}(2-\\sqrt{x})\\sqrt{4x+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242108}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13c6828e-9aa5-57a5-898e-af3e5b35e609" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}(2-\\sqrt{x})\\sqrt{\\frac{4x+1}{x}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241347} :answer-field/id #uuid "9037fb91-85b3-5449-8a77-c6e8b5996ac8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243707}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8775e28e-b78f-5d9e-8282-429406b40780" :question/math-academy-id "q-109143" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=2-\\sqrt{x}$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=4.$ What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${x}_{1}=0$ to ${x}_{2}=4.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2-\\sqrt{x})=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}(2-\\sqrt{x})\\sqrt{1+{(-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}(2-\\sqrt{x})\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}(2-\\sqrt{x})\\sqrt{\\frac{4x+1}{x}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244239 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0631f001-9df9-51cc-90ca-207a4ec9d888" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241234} {:answer/id #uuid "07898e0a-df78-56eb-948b-d6624803c46f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241242} {:answer/id #uuid "514bec40-43cf-5921-b0f1-a5f625af7922" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241907} {:answer/id #uuid "dd352c42-de2c-5892-a3e6-2f07f36b728e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243149} {:answer/id #uuid "fefc7f0b-937f-5705-8e05-726d03e28905" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243425}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0631f001-9df9-51cc-90ca-207a4ec9d888" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241234} :answer-field/id #uuid "505d2914-6e4a-5059-95e6-b60f71fd8484" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243595}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f4792eb7-cef8-5807-bfe2-869a28aaef03" :question/math-academy-id "q-109152" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,\\,1)$ for the curve $5{x}^{3}-2{y}^{4}=3.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{x}^{3}-2{y}^{4}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{x}^{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{y}^{4}) & =0 \\\\ 15{x}^{2}-8{y}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ -8{y}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-15{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{15{x}^{2}}{8{y}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(1,\\,1)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}_{(1,\\,1)} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{15{x}^{2}}{8{y}^{3}})}_{(1,\\,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{15\\cdot {1}^{2}}{8\\cdot {1}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244240 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05dccc3a-055f-56b3-b883-95c2ad00bc81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5x}{C+{x}^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241230} {:answer/id #uuid "086185d1-e93f-51d1-8b6d-eaa525ad35a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{C-5{x}^{5/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241252} {:answer/id #uuid "1d85751b-a7a9-5fd6-a5c7-c8956884f0d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5x}{C-2{x}^{5/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241430} {:answer/id #uuid "92878b28-57d2-57f6-8d59-8df96602fa63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{2x}{C-{x}^{3/2}})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242465} {:answer/id #uuid "e5d5a33a-0925-57f4-811a-6f47f2738dcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{x}{C+{x}^{5/2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243221}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d85751b-a7a9-5fd6-a5c7-c8956884f0d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5x}{C-2{x}^{5/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241430} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b754363-aaef-5ee9-923d-a786dd88ca8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243678}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "48fa1d56-7545-55a4-8175-861ef17502b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-109154" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{y}{x}=\\sqrt{x}\\,{y}^{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-\\frac{1}{x},\\,q(x)=\\sqrt{x},\\,n=2.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-2}\\frac{dy}{dx}-\\frac{1}{x}{y}^{-1}=\\sqrt{x}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{x}z=\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z=-\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,Q(x)=-\\sqrt{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=x$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z) & =x\\cdot (-\\sqrt{x}) \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z & =-x\\sqrt{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz) & =-{x}^{3/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -{x}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ xz & =-\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{C-2{x}^{5/2}}{5x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=5{C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-1}=\\frac{C-2{x}^{5/2}}{5x}\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{5x}{C-2{x}^{5/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244241 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "019d8c87-298f-5c5b-b237-edabb4429a26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}}{y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241200} {:answer/id #uuid "243ed89e-1499-5cf0-804c-486e1e217b23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241495} {:answer/id #uuid "64e696a9-d631-5093-a76e-db90aac429e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242087} {:answer/id #uuid "8053e264-cba1-54c1-abf5-c2f6089084cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242305} {:answer/id #uuid "c94f8ca1-3b69-5359-8da9-9840c31575e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242950}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c94f8ca1-3b69-5359-8da9-9840c31575e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242950} :answer-field/id #uuid "e33f6639-c2bd-59e0-864f-fb1a0c8eccca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243831}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "99e4906b-165d-530b-99fc-6645c7d97282" :question/math-academy-id "q-109155" :question/problem "![](images/22/22bb16a874005f09d5cbad055f149f07e2139f559a2a04368c32696567714b26.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x=\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}$ that lies in the first quadrant between the line $y=0$ and its $y$-intercept. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =2 \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=\\sqrt{2}.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}y\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}y\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{2-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244242 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18368760-9ddd-590c-8504-ac684d4a7552" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15{y}^{2}-20y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241390} {:answer/id #uuid "3e303b8b-f1ac-5a66-9423-bea9710de798" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3y-20{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241735} {:answer/id #uuid "51a97498-2ce1-56ec-8f2d-31e65df70135" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}-10y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241909} {:answer/id #uuid "6b0d0145-cf55-590a-b3f5-a04fac5deec6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15{y}^{2}-20y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242141} {:answer/id #uuid "ed51ff09-6401-520a-8e1b-522db433c5bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15y-10{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243278}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b0d0145-cf55-590a-b3f5-a04fac5deec6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15{y}^{2}-20y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242141} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfbc73a4-587b-5578-95db-03bfd926635a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243803}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2e657054-5587-5750-9021-605c700e3136" :question/math-academy-id "q-109156" :question/problem "If $x=5{y}^{3}-10{y}^{2},$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(5{y}^{3}-10{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =5\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{3})-10\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2}) \\\\  & =15{y}^{2}-20y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{15{y}^{2}-20y}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244246 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17db3d82-11ec-509d-ab63-0e5193af0c88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{{x}^{5/4}+C}{5{x}^{1/4}})}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241383} {:answer/id #uuid "2c0d3fc8-af2a-5397-a40e-b88eb47a52b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{C{x}^{1/2}-2}{4{x}^{1/2}})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241573} {:answer/id #uuid "601be5e6-b943-5b1d-bccd-fe6ec7de5ec8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{1/4}+C}{4{x}^{9/4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242043} {:answer/id #uuid "7e50f3de-2289-5189-ab71-28ca20cc56e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{{x}^{9/4}+C}{9{x}^{1/4}})}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242289} {:answer/id #uuid "e31a0853-d5e3-5909-a9a1-0d81e266fab8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{4{x}^{5/4}+C}{3{x}^{1/4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243201}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e50f3de-2289-5189-ab71-28ca20cc56e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{{x}^{9/4}+C}{9{x}^{1/4}})}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242289} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f084e80-d302-55b4-bd36-6495164f8e07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243525}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ad4ebdc1-705f-579a-b327-9d9638977692" :question/math-academy-id "q-109170" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\nx\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y={x}^{2}{y}^{3/4}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y={x}^{2}{y}^{3/4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{y}{x} & =x{y}^{3/4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{-1},\\,q(x)=x,\\,n=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{3/4},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-3/4}\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{-1}{y}^{1/4}=x.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{1/4}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{-3/4}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-3/4}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n4\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{-1}z=x\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-1}z=\\frac{x}{4}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is now in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{1}{4x},\\,Q(x)=\\frac{x}{4}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1/4x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{(1/4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={x}^{1/4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={x}^{1/4}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{1/4}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-1}z) & ={x}^{1/4}\\cdot (\\frac{x}{4}) \\\\ {x}^{1/4}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/4}z & =\\frac{{x}^{5/4}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{x}^{1/4}) & =\\frac{{x}^{5/4}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{x}^{1/4})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{{x}^{5/4}}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z{x}^{1/4} & =\\frac{1}{9}{x}^{9/4}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{{x}^{9/4}+C}{9{x}^{1/4}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=9{C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{1/4}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n{y}^{1/4}=\\frac{{x}^{9/4}+C}{9{x}^{1/4}}\\,⇒\\,y={(\\frac{{x}^{9/4}+C}{9{x}^{1/4}})}^{4}\\,.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244248 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "177d975d-e56e-5b9c-9d15-f202564d2dcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{x}^{2/3}-Cx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241381} {:answer/id #uuid "2494ed9c-553b-5032-82dd-4cad1100915e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{(3\\sqrt{x}+C)}^{2/3}}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241497} {:answer/id #uuid "2a0262c3-0c88-53c2-8f6c-a696ff2d9c86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{(3+Cx)}^{2/3}}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241549} {:answer/id #uuid "3ed47f22-7be9-5d13-aafa-9aa435553945" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3\\sqrt{x}+C{x}^{2/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241740} {:answer/id #uuid "b691ba50-56e8-5e37-96ad-51102a634da5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}+C}{x})}^{2/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242778}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2494ed9c-553b-5032-82dd-4cad1100915e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{(3\\sqrt{x}+C)}^{2/3}}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241497} :answer-field/id #uuid "c21b45e9-28a2-59e4-bff7-31ca0f9bf671" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7c7c063b-3c69-5ffc-9eea-18aa3a923ee9" :question/math-academy-id "q-109177" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}-xy.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={y}^{-1/2}-xy \\\\ {x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+xy & ={y}^{-1/2} \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{-1}y & ={x}^{-2}{y}^{-1/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{-1},\\,q(x)={x}^{-2},\\,n=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the differential equation by ${y}^{1/2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{1/2}\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{-1}{y}^{3/2}={x}^{-2}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{3/2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{1/2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{1/2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{-1}z={x}^{-2}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-1}z=\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-2}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is now in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{3}{2x},\\,Q(x)=\\frac{3}{2{x}^{2}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (3/2x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{(3/2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={x}^{3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={x}^{3/2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3/2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-1}z) & ={x}^{3/2}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-2}) \\\\ {x}^{3/2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{1/2}z & =\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3/2}z) & =\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3/2}z)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {x}^{3/2}z & =3{x}^{1/2}+C \\\\ z & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{x}+C}{{x}^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{3/2}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n{y}^{3/2}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{x}+C}{{x}^{3/2}}\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{{(3\\sqrt{x}+C)}^{2/3}}{x}\\,.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244249 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f907e53-3694-5ad0-b3c9-88309072c90b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}y\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241606} {:answer/id #uuid "40c7ea4c-b252-545c-a5a2-7261c7b45c1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{e}y\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241759} {:answer/id #uuid "6dfb8f9a-8e9e-56c9-9ba7-ca746857dc6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242161} {:answer/id #uuid "9dae0167-4d88-5f41-831d-f1919cbde292" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{e}\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242557} {:answer/id #uuid "adbec8e1-4fb0-5736-b8c5-6bc930339f60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\sqrt{y({y}^{2}+1)}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242697}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6dfb8f9a-8e9e-56c9-9ba7-ca746857dc6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242161} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c2deca4-99b0-5aa4-b569-6152add0f28f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243519}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e38dda82-9f39-5297-8b68-eff9a87b31e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-109188" :question/problem "![](images/9b/9b27f44aa7ef6bef95a88ccfcb6495d17e9457a7452bf602f87dcd5b95ec71dc.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ that lies in the first quadrant between its $y$-intercept and the line $y=e.$ What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y} & ={e}^{0} \\\\ y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=1$ to $y=e.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=\\frac{1}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{1}{y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}y\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244252 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e8f53e1-c79d-5f14-a4e3-fac06695a1d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}y\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241447} {:answer/id #uuid "365a8d34-cc0c-5806-a5af-b486c8fe068e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241657} {:answer/id #uuid "a0b8a636-5e0d-5aaa-be3e-890c04b5201d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}}{y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242585} {:answer/id #uuid "c56c1734-c941-54bf-8e79-acc9476011c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}}{y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242922} {:answer/id #uuid "ee432a6b-abe0-524a-b89f-b7d5bc2259a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243292}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0b8a636-5e0d-5aaa-be3e-890c04b5201d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}}{y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242585} :answer-field/id #uuid "79f40067-a4d4-5d1a-9dbc-ac0be82d20e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243674}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3955735d-411f-53ca-9d80-4ffea69537dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-109237" :question/problem "![](images/2b/2baa8f39370b831bd8306aa3eb60e5d16d54ce50474f873f0fa329c069d61689.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ that lies between $y=1$ and $y=e,$ as shown above. What integral gives the area of the surface generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $y=1$ to $y=e.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=\\frac{1}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{1}{y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{{y}^{2}+1}{{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}}{y}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244255 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15a0605f-0d98-5356-87d0-f15f078952b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{6}(y-4)\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}-32y+65}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241365} {:answer/id #uuid "4d254a9b-5e4c-5210-93c4-cd7fd2e53fd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+6}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241872} {:answer/id #uuid "81d1ee44-6fd3-51a4-b9b6-5bb1916fc469" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{6}(-{y}^{2}+8y-12)\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}-32y+65}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242318} {:answer/id #uuid "866b8c48-5e44-50dc-b4e5-159d11f20022" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{6}(y-4)\\sqrt{{y}^{2}-2y+5}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242358} {:answer/id #uuid "940a750f-f441-5d5b-9397-f0841d6938f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}(-{y}^{2}+8y-12)\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+6}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81d1ee44-6fd3-51a4-b9b6-5bb1916fc469" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{6}(-{y}^{2}+8y-12)\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}-32y+65}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242318} :answer-field/id #uuid "7cf813a5-40a3-5244-8d74-4ccdeb3ff8b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243681}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eee8c8a0-68a0-5a7d-816a-fe17e450ddb5" :question/math-academy-id "q-109258" :question/problem "![](images/3d/3d01ddb2278875780397e90a9d6f6ad6b2fc44890c59faab7a69b8ebcb5d210b.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x=4-(y-4)^{2}$ that lies in the first quadrant between its $y$-intercepts. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-(y-4)^{2} & =0 \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =4 \\\\ y-4 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ y & =2,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=2$ to $y=6.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(4-(y-4)^{2})=8-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{6}(4-(y-4)^{2})\\sqrt{1+{(8-2y)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{6}(-{y}^{2}+8y-12)\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}-32y+65}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244256 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44baf867-9c47-5e3b-9202-8db171f73e3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{\\sqrt{3}}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241799} {:answer/id #uuid "685c2d43-d50a-565b-b549-e7b4edc4a543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242117} {:answer/id #uuid "c399fb16-6a09-5610-a6a0-6eafdd769db2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{\\sqrt{3}}{x}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242898} {:answer/id #uuid "e6c1800f-23e4-59e7-84be-0447eb685ffd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243227} {:answer/id #uuid "ff6d54b8-03f1-53e6-ac46-511caa5ac279" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243428}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "685c2d43-d50a-565b-b549-e7b4edc4a543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242117} :answer-field/id #uuid "83b4db92-018b-5f0d-b35a-417f7b2db39e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243688}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e36b6b25-8f88-5e08-b750-faf815fb6a2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-109261" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ that lies between the lines $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=\\sqrt{3}.$ What integral gives the area of the surface generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{\\sqrt{3}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{1}{x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{\\sqrt{3}}x\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244273 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f114d1a-49df-5d70-b382-c1b34ced3034" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi (2-\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241308} {:answer/id #uuid "15e97cef-a6a1-5c7a-826e-a413d825e1c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}(2-\\sqrt{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241368} {:answer/id #uuid "73485071-9dc6-51f6-951d-1f761b586828" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}(5-\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242203} {:answer/id #uuid "c5f9087f-e565-5ba5-83cb-5857fa4cd226" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi (6-\\sqrt{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242924} {:answer/id #uuid "ceff91c3-fb7b-5230-bcfc-28c572a8b5ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi (3-\\sqrt{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243003}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ceff91c3-fb7b-5230-bcfc-28c572a8b5ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi (3-\\sqrt{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243003} :answer-field/id #uuid "355ab9f5-c4e4-5b46-8078-422005344320" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243538}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c817dfb7-cfc8-5d1c-9306-1a0f55b185a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-109319" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $x=\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}$ that lies between the lines $y=0$ and $y=2.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${y}_{1}=0$ to ${y}_{2}=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}}\\,⇒\\,{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9-{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{9-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\frac{3y}{\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =3\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{2y}{\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the substitution \n\n$$\nu=9-{y}^{2}\\,⇒\\,\\text{d}u=-2y\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =3\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{2y}{\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-3\\pi {\\int }_{9}^{5}{u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\pi {\\int }_{5}^{9}{u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\pi {[2\\sqrt{u}\\,]}_{5}^{9} \\\\  & =6\\pi (3-\\sqrt{5}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244281 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27ee5025-4ebd-5f5a-b5e4-09b9ead9ca29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241528} {:answer/id #uuid "5949ca32-c191-5120-9f04-4ac30b8e563a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241984} {:answer/id #uuid "b3153140-5ee9-5209-8c08-5a8f698db225" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242739} {:answer/id #uuid "ce5717ac-5b74-529f-8168-42712e86525b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242996} {:answer/id #uuid "e051ef75-4ccb-5426-89e1-8839c66a7e41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27ee5025-4ebd-5f5a-b5e4-09b9ead9ca29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241528} :answer-field/id #uuid "67fbff3a-f876-5971-a21c-709b88cf04be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243645}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f162f698-8220-54f4-a16d-68bf7f2cabee" :question/math-academy-id "q-109373" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,J=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & a \\\\ 1 & b\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $2a+2b.$" :question/worked-solution "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that \n\n- the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2,$\n\n- the second column of $P$ corresponds to the vector ${v}_{2}$ such that $(A-2I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}.$\n\nNow, computing $A-2I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}-(2)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(A-2I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get  \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $2a+2b=2\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{2})+2\\cdot 1=-1.$"}]
